<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Usfurid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1253 CE – 1330 CE (approx.) · capital al-Ahsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bedouin dynasty of the Banu Uqayl that replaced the Uyunids in eastern Arabia and controlled the al-Ahsa oasis in the 13th and 14th centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Usfurids, named after their ancestor Usfur ibn Rashid, were a clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-amir">Banu Amir</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uqaylid-dynasty">Banu Uqayl</a> tribe. Around 1253 they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Ahsa</a> from the weakened <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uyunid-emirate">Uyunid Emirate</a> and became the leading power of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their power rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribal forces and on the revenues of the date oases and the pilgrimage and trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a>. They maintained links with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in Iraq and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and competed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Kingdom of Ormus</a> for control of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a> islands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> ports. Sources on them are thin.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 14th century the Usfurids lost the islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> to rulers allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Hormuz</a>, and by about 1330 their authority had passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jarwanid-dynasty">Jarwanid dynasty</a> and other local leaders.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1253 CE – 1330 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.64%;width:1.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1253 CE – 1330 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>al-Ahsa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/bahrain">Bahrain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Usfurids, Banu Usfur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1292&amp;focus=usfurid-dynasty" aria-label="Usfurid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5867 1313 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Usfurid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6318 1458h0M6485 1440h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6187 1569h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6187 1569h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Saudi Arabia. <a href="/atlas?year=1292&amp;focus=usfurid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
